00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:16.350 align:start position:0%
flying insects are some of the most  agile maneuverable creatures on earth  able to fly in any direction almost  instantaneously their aerial agility  allows them to avoid predators and land  on moving objects with precision  airplanes and helicopters embodies some  of the maneuverability of flies but  miniaturizing these machines to the size  of insects presents significant  technical challenges in control design  manufacturing and power delivery now a  team of scientists at Harvard University  has developed a lightweight insect scale  flying robot that mimics the form and  function of flies an engineering  achievement in the meso scale which is  too large for microscopic electronics  and too small for conventional macro  scale manufacturing and assembly  weighing in at just 80 milligrams and  with a wingspan of three centimeters  this tiny flyer receives power and  control via a tether made of lightweight  carbon fiber and thin plastics all of  its components are designed to be  mass-produced the flapping of the wings  is generated by piezoelectric muscles  which move back and forth in response to  changes in voltage the wings also rotate  forward and back like shoulders rolling  in their sockets which generates  downward thrust and enables the robot to  fly each wing flaps and rotates  independently allowing the robot to  immediately self correct its position  and orientation the wings can flap at  nearly the same frequency as flies of  comparable size approximately 120 beats  per second like an insect  this flying robot has the ability to  hover and move laterally between  discrete points in space it has  maintained upwards of 20 seconds of  stable sustained flight should robotic  flies become easily reproducible these  devices may provide a new medium for  exploring the mechanics of insect flight  as well as inspiring the development of  miniaturized power sensing and  computational technologies  you
 

